 **Wake to admission.** `supervisor wake` -> no live supervisor for this project: start one under `head:supervisor-<handle>`; a live one only sees the queue -> supervisor drains the queue -> admits `ready` plans by priority while leads < `supervisor.maxLeads` and a lane is free -> starts one lead per plan -> `supervisor done` on an empty queue ends its session.
 
-**Lead to land.** Lead runs `fgos run --writes <footprint paths>` in a linked worktree -> reviews the phase -> `fgos land`: fetch, prepare (holding the target's merge slot, rank 20, across the gate), run `land.gates` and `land.regenerate` (which may carry `when` globs, like a gate, and then runs only if a landed path matches) in the candidate, push the candidate's pin ref, publish (fast-forward of local main); retries 3 times when the remote moved. A refusal after prepare (gate, regeneration, conflict, push) settles the door's own Land operation through the Git ports (recover, then release the candidate and pins), so the next `fgos land` is not blocked, and the candidate checkout goes with it, ignored build output included (Git spec, retained Land candidates); a refusal always names its own code and detail first, and trouble releasing the candidate follows as a separate `cleanup note` line (`LandRefusal.cleanup_note`), never replacing it; only a crash leaves `fgos git recover` to run. A failed gate or regeneration keeps its full stdout+stderr in `.fgos/runtime/land/<operation id>.log`; the refusal names that path and shows the lines that name failures (`not ok`, `✖`, `ℹ fail`, `test ... FAILED`, `failures:`, `error[`), else the last 4 KiB. Land merges, never rebases. A conflict on a declared generated file is regenerated in the candidate and prepared again with a fresh operation id. A `--paths` land holds the same merge slot across its sync, gates, commit and push (a live Land of the target makes it wait, then refuse `merge-slot-busy`); its gates run in the main checkout before the commit and intentionally see other dirty files there.
